# Who is asked to review what.
#
# The list is deliberately short. Only the *structural* files have an owner —
# the ones where a single change affects every page on the site at once. A
# recipe is additive and self-contained, so `_recipes/` and the recipe
# photographs are left unowned on purpose: a recipe contribution should not
# wait on one person being available.
#
# Note what this file does and does not do. On its own, CODEOWNERS only
# *requests* a review. It becomes a requirement when "Require review from Code
# Owners" is switched on in branch protection — see #40.
#
# Later patterns win over earlier ones, so order matters: put the general rule
# first and the exception after it.

# The layouts generate the JSON-LD that every importer reads. A mistake here is
# not visible on the page — it breaks the import silently, on all of them.
/_layouts/                @digvijaygunjal
/_includes/               @digvijaygunjal

# The allergen list. The layout derives each recipe's "free from" list by
# subtracting what the recipe declares from this file, so editing it changes
# what every published recipe claims about itself.
/_data/                   @digvijaygunjal

# Site settings, the appliance every recipe is timed against, and the
# collection configuration that decides the URLs.
/_config.yml              @digvijaygunjal

# Issue forms, the pull request template, this file, and any workflow added
# later.
/.github/                 @digvijaygunjal

# Deliberately unowned, and this is the point of the file rather than an
# oversight:
#
#   /_recipes/            — a new recipe is additive; nothing else changes
#   /assets/images/       — likewise
#
# When the test suite lands (#32, #33, #34, #36), add it here. A test that can
# be weakened without review is not a test.
